My favorite next denominator: n=18
  This is 18 total runners in the repo convention, or 17 moving speeds against a stationary runner.
  Why: it is still computationally close to 14, but it is not just the same anomaly repeated.
  Its lpd ladder uses d=9, has gap/th=0.005682, unprotected=176, and pressure/n^2=95.604938.
  Structurally, 18=2*3^2 combines an even gate, a square 3-torsion layer, and a largest-proper-divisor layer 9.  That gives both a clean quotient ladder and a CRT/torsion interface.
  Its product-sum comparison is m(18)=24 from seed (2, 3, 4) with 15 ones, so the natural-operation side is already multi-factor rather than purely binary.
  My read: n=16 is the clean proof laboratory, n=21 is the pretty 3-by-7 transfer, and n=24 is the stress test.  But n=18 is the best next battlefield: small enough to certify, rich enough to expose the recursive mechanism.
